'''Mutsu Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|陸奥県}}, ''Mutsu-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region on the southwestern tip of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the south and west. With a population of 544,978 as of 2026 spread across 9,703 km², it is the second-largest prefecture on Yagikoshu by area and one of the most sparsely populated. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Mutsu]], a port and dairy-processing centre.

Mutsu Prefecture is Okaiken's dairy heartland—the centre of the nation's milk, cheese, and butter production—and the site of the {{nihongo|Mutsu Wind Corridor|陸奥風回廊|Mutsu Kaze Kairō}}, the largest onshore wind energy installation in the Shendan Ocean region. The prefecture's cool, open grasslands and persistent ocean winds create a landscape that is unique in Yagikoshu, more reminiscent of the northern latitudes than of the subtropical island it occupies.

== History ==

=== The last frontier ===
Mutsu was the last region of Yagikoshu to be settled by the Sanese, and its development followed a fundamentally different trajectory from the rest of the island. While the northern and eastern coasts were settled by fishing and trading communities from the earliest periods, the southwestern tip of Yagikoshu—exposed to the full force of the Shendan Ocean, with poor natural harbours and thin volcanic soils—remained sparsely populated until the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1868.

The Restoration government identified Mutsu as a potential site for large-scale agricultural colonisation, modelled on the {{nihongo|''kaitakushi''|開拓使}} colonisation programme. Government-sponsored settlers from the more crowded northern prefectures were offered land grants in the Mutsu grasslands, and the prefecture's dairy industry was established in the 1890s with the import of Holstein cattle and the construction of the first creameries.

=== The dairy revolution ===
Mutsu's dairy industry grew rapidly in the 20th century, driven by the prefecture's abundant pastureland and the national government's policy of dairy self-sufficiency. By the 1960s, Mutsu was producing approximately 40% of Okaiken's domestic milk supply, and the {{nihongo|Mutsu Butter|陸奥バター|Mutsu Batā}} and {{nihongo|Mutsu Cheese|陸奥チーズ|Mutsu Chīzu}} brands had become household names across the archipelago.

The industry faced a severe crisis in the 1990s Restructuring Era, when trade liberalisation allowed cheaper imported dairy products to compete with domestic production. The prefecture responded by moving upmarket, developing artisanal cheese varieties and premium dairy products that could command higher prices. The {{nihongo|Mutsu Artisanal Cheese Association|陸奥手工チーズ協会|Mutsu Shukō Chīzu Kyōkai}}, established in 1998, now produces over 60 varieties of cheese, several of which have won international awards.

== Geography ==

Mutsu Prefecture occupies the southwestern tip of Yagikoshu, facing the Shendan Ocean to the south and west. The prefecture is bounded by [[Uruwa Prefecture]] to the east, [[Mizuno Prefecture]] to the northeast, and the open ocean to the south and west. The prefecture's geography is defined by its extensive grasslands—the {{nihongo|Mutsu Plains|陸奥平原|Mutsu Heigen}}—which cover approximately 40% of the prefecture's area, making it the only region of Yagikoshu with significant natural pasture.

The [[Mutsu Mountains]] form the prefecture's eastern boundary, rising to over 1,800 metres and creating a rain shadow that makes the western plains significantly drier than the rest of Yagikoshu. The coastline is characterised by long, sweeping beaches and dramatic sea cliffs, with few natural harbours—a geography that has limited the development of the fishing industry.

=== Climate ===
Mutsu Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa'') that is significantly cooler and drier than the rest of Yagikoshu due to the rain shadow effect and the persistent ocean winds. Summer temperatures typically reach 26–29°C, while winter temperatures can fall to near freezing in the highland areas. The prefecture receives approximately 1,800 mm of annual rainfall—less than half of Kagami Prefecture on the opposite coast—making it the driest prefecture on Yagikoshu.

The persistent winds—averaging 7.2 m/s annually, the highest in Okaiken—have shaped the prefecture's architecture, agriculture, and energy infrastructure.

