== Economy ==

Mutsu Prefecture's economy is driven by dairy farming, wind energy, and the associated processing industries. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円18 billion in 2025, with agriculture (primarily dairy) accounting for 32%, industry (dairy processing and wind energy) 30%, and services 38%.

=== Dairy farming ===
[[File:Shiranui Blue Cheese.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Shiranui Blue, a pungent blue cheese that has won three gold medals at the Adonian Cheese Awards]]
[[File:Mutsu Dairy Farm.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A Mutsu dairy farm on the open grasslands]]
The {{nihongo|Mutsu Dairy Belt|陸奥酪農帯|Mutsu Rakunō-tai}} covers approximately 180,000 hectares of the Mutsu Plains, operating approximately 2,400 dairy farms that collectively maintain approximately 420,000 head of Holstein cattle. The prefecture produces approximately 3.2 million tonnes of milk annually—roughly 35% of Okaiken's domestic production—and is the nation's leading producer of butter, cheese, and yoghurt.

The {{nihongo|Mutsu Artisanal Cheese Association|陸奥手工チーズ協会|Mutsu Shukō Chīzu Kyōkai}} produces over 60 varieties of cheese, including the {{nihongo|''Mutsu Gouda''|陸奥ゴーダ|Mutsu Gōda}}, the {{nihongo|''Mutsu Camembert''|陸奥カマンベール|Mutsu Kamanbēru}}, and the {{nihongo|''Shiranui Blue''|不知火ブルー|Shiranui Burū}}—a pungent blue cheese that has won three gold medals at the [[Adonian Cheese Awards]]. The dairy industry employs approximately 15,000 workers and generates approximately 円8 billion in annual revenue.

=== Wind energy ===
[[File:Mutsu Wind Corridor.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Mutsu Wind Corridor, the largest onshore wind energy installation in the Shendan Ocean region]]
The {{nihongo|Mutsu Wind Corridor|陸奥風回廊|Mutsu Kaze Kairō}} is the largest onshore wind energy installation in the Shendan Ocean region, with 420 turbines generating approximately 2.8 GW of electricity—enough to power the entire prefecture with significant surplus exported to the national grid. The Corridor was developed in three phases between 2008 and 2024, and its expansion is ongoing.

The wind energy sector employs approximately 2,200 workers in maintenance, operations, and turbine manufacturing. The {{nihongo|Mutsu Wind Technology Centre|陸奥風技術センター|Mutsu Kaze Gijutsu Sentā}} conducts research into next-generation turbine designs and grid integration, and the prefecture has become a testbed for experimental wind energy technologies.

=== Ocean fishing ===
Mutsu's fishing industry is smaller than those of other Shendan coast prefectures, limited by the lack of natural harbours. However, the {{nihongo|Shizunami|静波}} and [[Nagisa]] fleets land approximately 35,000 tonnes of seafood annually, primarily {{nihongo|sanma|秋刀魚}} (Pacific saury), {{nihongo|hokke|ホッケ}} (Atka mackerel), and various deep-sea species.
Mutsu's northern maritime climate supports commercial dairy farming, renewable wind generation, and traditional cold-weather fish preservation. Coastal wind farms harness steady offshore gales to generate substantial clean electricity for the northern grid. In the agriculture sector, cooperative dairy farms produce high-grade butter, cheese, and milk, while coastal fishing villages practice saba kanzō—packing fresh winter mackerel in salt and clean snowdrifts to cure at freezing temperatures, creating sweet, tender cured fish delicacies.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Mutsu Line]] connects [[Mutsu Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 200 minutes on express services, the longest rail journey of any prefectural capital on Yagikoshu.

=== Air ===
[[Mutsu Airport]] (IATA: MTU) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Mutsu]] handles approximately 3 million tonnes of cargo annually, primarily dairy exports and wind energy equipment.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Mutsu's cuisine is defined by its dairy and its cool climate. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Mutsu cheese nabe''|陸奥チーズ鍋|Mutsu Chīzu Nabe}}—a rich, creamy hot pot made with Mutsu cheese, local vegetables, and pork or chicken. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''Mutsu butter ramen''|陸奥バターラーメン|Mutsu Batā Rāmen}} (ramen in a butter-rich broth), {{nihongo|''sanma no shioyaki''|秋刀魚の塩焼き}} (grilled Pacific saury), and the various artisanal cheeses of the Mutsu Cheese Association.

The {{nihongo|Mutsu Soft Serve|陸奥ソフトクリーム|Mutsu Sofuto Kurīmu}}—soft-serve ice cream made with fresh Mutsu milk—is a ubiquitous roadside treat, sold at approximately 200 dairy stands across the prefecture.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Mutsu Cheese Festival'''|陸奥チーズ祭り|Mutsu Chīzu Matsuri}} — Annual cheese festival (October), drawing approximately 60,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Wind Festival'''|風祭り|Kaze Matsuri}} — Annual celebration of the wind energy industry (September), with kite-flying and turbine tours
* {{nihongo|'''Shiranui Fire Festival'''|不知火火祭り|Shiranui Hi Matsuri}} — Winter solstice bonfire festival (June), named for the mysterious "Shiranui" fire lights seen on the horizon

